<figure class="lyc">
  <div class="lyc-buttons">
    <button data-id="front">front-lit</button>
    <button data-id="back">back-lit</button>
  </div>
  <div class="lyc-stage">
    <div class="lyc-cup" role="img" aria-label="computed colors of the Lycurgus Cup glass"></div>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="lyc-caption"></figcaption>
  <p class="island-footnote">Computed, not painted: Mie theory for 70:30 silver-gold spheres 60 nm across in glass, with the alloy's optical constants from Rioux et al. (2014) and the metal content Freestone et al. (2007) report. How much of that metal is in particles (6% here) and the wall thickness (5 mm) are assumptions, and the front-lit view counts light scattered once. The model shows a tension: put more metal in particles and the back-lit red deepens toward the cup's wine red, but the front-lit view drifts from green to khaki. It cannot give both at once, so something it leaves out matters (multiple scattering, the particles' real shapes and sizes, or other phases in the glass). The real cup is in the British Museum.</p>
</figure>
<script>
  import { initLycurgusFlip } from '../../scripts/islands/lycurgus.js';
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll('.lyc')) initLycurgusFlip(el);
</script>
